---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const description =
  'Who writes CalcPulse, how the figures are checked, and what the site deliberately does not do.';
---

<BaseLayout title="About CalcPulse" description={description}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">
    <nav class="text-sm text-ink-400" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-ink-600">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> About
    </nav>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-950">About CalcPulse</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-ink mt-6">
      <p>
        CalcPulse is a small, independent site. It publishes one kind of page: a decision guide with a
        calculator on it. The topic range is narrow on purpose — AI running costs, automation tooling,
        and the small-business finance questions that sit next to them.
      </p>

      <h2>How a page gets written</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The arithmetic first.</strong> The calculator is built before the prose. If the
          formula cannot be defended line by line, the page does not get published.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rates are inputs, not claims.</strong> Where a figure changes often — token prices,
          payout thresholds, platform fees — the page takes your number instead of printing one that
          will be stale in a month. Where a figure is quoted, it is dated.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Assumptions are listed.</strong> Each guide ends with what it leaves out. A guide
          that hides its boundary is selling rather than explaining.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What this site will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No email gate, no sign-up, no account to use a calculator.</li>
        <li>No calculator that sends your inputs to a server. The maths runs in your browser.</li>
        <li>
          No thousands of near-identical pages generated per keyword. Two useful pages beat two
          hundred thin ones, and thin pages are what search engines are quietly removing.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Guides carry a “last reviewed” date. When a source changes or a reader points out an error, the
        page is corrected and the date moves. If you find something wrong, the fix matters more than
        the wording of the complaint — send the source and the page will be updated.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>
        Some pages may carry affiliate links to tools discussed on them. That never determines whether
        a tool appears, and a page that says a tool is a poor fit for a scenario will still link to it
        with that warning attached. See the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">disclosure</a> for detail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
